        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains November 28 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, such as 2 of 5.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For November 28 in music history, the Hot 100 chart week that includes November 28, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the longest run, with ten weeks starting October 15, 1977.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and three were in the final week of theirs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the November 28 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called November 28.</p>
        <p>This page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains November 28 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">November 28 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A TV Family, a Movie Theme and a Four-Week Climb</h2>
        <p>The first three Novembers of the decade were led by a television group, a film theme and a pop favorite.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I Think I Love You" by the Partridge Family</h3>
        <p>Tony Romeo wrote "I Think I Love You," and it was released on August 22, 1970, about a month before the TV series debuted.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it topped the Hot 100 for three weeks in November and December 1970.</p>
        <p>The RIAA certified the single Gold for a million units.</p>
        <p>The run began November 21, and November 28 was the second week.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Theme from Shaft" by Isaac Hayes</h3>
        <p>Isaac Hayes wrote and produced "Theme from Shaft," and it came from his 1971 album Shaft.</p>
        <p>It won the 1972 Academy Award for Best Original Song.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Hayes was the first African American to win an Academy Award in a non-acting category.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one, and November 27 was the second and last.</p>

        <h3>1972: "I Can See Clearly Now" by Johnny Nash</h3>
        <p>"I Can See Clearly Now" was the lead single from Johnny Nash's album of the same name.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it took only two weeks to vault from number 20 to number 5 to number 1.</p>
        <p>It also spent the same four weeks at number one on the Hot Adult Contemporary Tracks chart.</p>
        <p>It topped the Hot 100 from November 4 to November 25, and November 25 was the fourth and last week.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">November 28 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Beatle, a Country Crossover and a Disco Double Run</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a solo Beatle, a one-hit country-pop favorite and a disco band that hit the top twice.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Photograph" by Ringo Starr</h3>
        <p>Ringo Starr and George Harrison wrote "Photograph," and it is the only song officially credited to the pair.</p>
        <p>Richard Perry produced it for Starr's 1973 album Ringo.</p>
        <p>It was Starr's first solo number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia reports that Cilla Black wanted to record it, and Starr said no.</p>
        <p>It held the top for one week, starting November 24.</p>

        <h3>1974: "I Can Help" by Billy Swan</h3>
        <p>Billy Swan wrote and sang "I Can Help," and he produced it with Chip Young.</p>
        <p>Swan wrote the song using a "Rock" preset on his Rhythm Master drum machine, according to Wikipedia.</p>
        <p>It also reached number one on the Hot Country Singles chart in November 1974.</p>
        <p>It held the top for two weeks, starting November 23.</p>
        <p>The song is number 44 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Swan also appears on the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-one-hit-wonders/">70s one-hit wonders</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1975: "That's the Way (I Like It)" by KC and the Sunshine Band</h3>
        <p>Harry Wayne Casey and Richard Finch wrote and produced "That's the Way (I Like It)" for the band's second studio album.</p>
        <p>It was the band's second number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>The week of November 22 was its first, and then Silver Convention's "Fly, Robin, Fly" took over for three weeks.</p>
        <p>The single came back for a second week starting December 20, which Wikipedia calls rare for a chart-topper within one month.</p>
        <p>The song is number 45 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the band's hits are on <a href="/blog/artists/kc-and-the-sunshine-band-songs/">KC and the Sunshine Band Songs</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="late">November 28 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Long Run, a Record, a Disco Epic and a First Duet</h2>
        <p>The last four Novembers brought the decade's longest run and two disco-era number ones in a row.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)" by Rod Stewart</h3>
        <p>Rod Stewart wrote "Tonight's the Night (Gonna Be Alright)," and Tom Dowd produced it for the album A Night on the Town.</p>
        <p>Britt Ekland, who was Stewart's girlfriend at the time, speaks the French part.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says the BBC originally banned it, and Reverend Jesse Jackson protested its suggestive lyrics.</p>
        <p>It held number one for eight weeks, from November 13, 1976 to January 1, 1977, and November 27 was the third.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>"You Light Up My Life" ran ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says that set a new Hot 100 record for most weeks at number one.</p>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote it, and Kasey Cisyk first recorded it for the film's soundtrack.</p>
        <p>November 26 was the seventh of the ten weeks.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "MacArthur Park" by Donna Summer</h3>
        <p>Jimmy Webb wrote "MacArthur Park," and Giorgio Moroder and Pete Bellotte produced Donna Summer's version.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says hers is the only recording of a Webb composition to top the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>It reached number one the week of November 11, and November 25 was the third and last of its three weeks.</p>
        <p>The full story is on <a href="/blog/songs/macarthur-park-donna-summer/">MacArthur Park by Donna Summer</a>.</p>
        <p>More of her records are on <a href="/blog/artists/donna-summer/">Donna Summer</a>.</p>
        <p>The song is number 12 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1979: "No More Tears (Enough Is Enough)" by Barbra Streisand and Donna Summer</h3>
        <p>Paul Jabara and Bruce Roberts wrote "No More Tears (Enough Is Enough)," and Gary Klein and Giorgio Moroder produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it the first duet by two women to top the Billboard Hot 100.</p>
        <p>The original title was "Enough Is Enough," and the writers changed it to fit the water theme of Streisand's album Wet.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one, starting November 24.</p>

